WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.001
-Welcome back to "Late Night,"

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.002
We're here with
Sterling K. Brown,

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.370
Justin Hartley and
Chrissy Metz.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.172
So I don't want to know anything
about the ending,

00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:08.875
but I'm curious, Chrissy,

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:12.545
how much have you been told
about the ending?

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.681
Did the show's creator,
Dan Fogelman --

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:17.517
does he lay it out for you?
-Oh, does he.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:18.851
I mean, we know everything.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.352
And I think he is really excited
to share it

00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:21.987
because it really is brilliant.

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:24.223
So I always get so excited
when he calls or he texts,

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:27.026
and we're like, "What?"
But, yeah, we know everything.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:31.097
-Now, do you know -- I guess,
so, Sterling, does everyone know

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.498
everything about
all the characters?

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:35.134
Or do you know secrets that your
other cast mates don't know

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:37.936
about how your character ends
things?

00:00:37.936 --> 00:00:40.039
-I may know some things
that other folks don't know.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:42.175
I used to go into
the writers' room all the time.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.010
That was one of my favorite
things, just to listen to them

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.180
make the sausage.
And I would be fully invested.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.018
Like, I think I knew about the
divorce before --

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:54.087
I'm not giving --
before Chrissy did.

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:56.422
And then I remember talking to
her when she found out,

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:59.492
and she was just crushed.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:00.926
-Nobody ever wants to hear

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:03.362
about their divorce
from a third party.

00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:06.532
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:08.034
Exactly.

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.837
-So, yeah, I asked Dan as much
as I possibly can,

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.039
and sit in the writers room,
and listen to them come up

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.208
with everyone's storylines,
and the detail that they have

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.611
for everybody has been second to
none from the beginning.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:21.580
It's -- they're so conscientious
about crafting these arcs

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.549
and finishing them off right.
-Well, and they are.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:27.053
They're, like, so finely tuned,
and so much of the show has been

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:30.089
this incredible ability to
maintain these twists and turns.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:33.192
As actors, did you always want
to know everything?

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:39.198
Or did you like to have some
surprise as it came?

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:42.735
-I wish I was cool enough to
have that in me,

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.271
that I wanted some surprise.
I always admired those actors.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.705
I thought it was so cool,
the actors that are like,

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:50.343
"I don't want to know." And
I'm -- I'm an idiot. I'm a dork.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:51.477
I'm, like,
I need to know everything.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:52.811
Give me all the information.
Help me out.

00:01:52.811 --> 00:01:56.715
So I always like to know.
-Well, we span so many years.

00:01:56.715 --> 00:01:58.885
So it's hard, you know,
like if there's something

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.153
the teenagers are going through
that maybe as adults we're like,

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:02.355
"Oh, wait, we
went through that."

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.957
Oh, that informs is something
else completely,

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:06.525
when we're doing,
you know, our stuff.

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.494
So I like to know.
I like to know.

00:02:08.494 --> 00:02:11.930
-I also think that, like, if
you're doing a play or a movie,

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.999
you get to know the beginning,
middle, and end

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:15.268
from the beginning.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.336
So why not, if you have the
opportunity in a TV show

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:20.340
to have a similar sort of, like,
information from the beginning?

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.708
-Yeah.
-And now, Justin,

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:25.645
are you ever -- do you ever have
family, friends,

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:29.181
try to get it out of you
before it's going to air?

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:31.784
-I used to.
I used to quite a bit.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:34.053
And how I put the kibosh
on that was --

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.389
this was a couple years ago,
when families could still

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:37.489
get together and all of that,

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:39.125
and my whole family was
together.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.327
And they were pressuring me,
pressuring me,

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.395
and it got so annoying.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.596
And I kept telling everyone,
I said,

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.664
"You think you want to know,
but you don't."

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:47.900
And they're like, "No, no, tell
me, tell me." So I told them.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.369
I just -- I crushed them.

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:51.137
I ruined the whole entire thing.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.340
I told them the entire thing,
and I said,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.708
"And now, you know, you can
choose to watch it or not,

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:56.975
but now you know
what's going to happen.

00:02:56.975 --> 00:02:58.410
Don't ever ask me again."
And so they've stopped.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.078
They've stopped asking me.

00:03:00.078 --> 00:03:03.383
They're afraid that I might
actually say something.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.852
-Now I -- you know, again,
we've talked about --

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.488
and, Sterling, you mentioned
you want to get out

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.257
while the gettin's good, while
people still love the show,

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:14.293
but you also appreciate
how important it is,

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:15.862
especially
with a show like this,

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:18.764
that has marvelled with its plot
over the years

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:20.033
to nail the landing.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:21.700
Have you talked to Dan
about the importance

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.070
of getting the landing right?
-Oh, bro, you know I have.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:30.743
Listen, one of my favorite shows
of all time, until it ended,

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:33.278
Seth, was a show called
"Game of Thrones."

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:34.614
-Oh, no.
[ Audience laughs ]

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.882
-And listen to me now
because I've seen you --

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:38.517
I've seen you talk about
"Game of Thrones" a lot.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.820
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-You would go back and --

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.155
I'd watch it from the beginning,
in preparation

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:44.757
for the next season, right?
-Yeah.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.827
-During the pandemic,
I had no desire to watch it,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.095
and that made me sad

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.630
because I loved
that flippin' show,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.199
but they didn't
stick the landing.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.668
And so, when I watched --
-You know there are, like,

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.803
10 different prequels of that
show right now

00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:00.039
that you could be cast in? And I
don't want you to blow this.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:02.875
[ Laughter ]
-Listen, I know Dan and Dave.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.610
I appreciate that.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.278
And they know --
I've said it enough.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:10.716
I think they know,
it just wasn't quite right.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.585
And so I watched the finale,
and I said,

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:14.686
"Dan, we got to do
better than this."

00:04:14.686 --> 00:04:18.023
And he texted back, "We will."
I was like, "Okay, good."

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:21.627
-Does that mean -- you're
promising me the finale

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:25.998
of "This Is Us" doesn't end with
Bran being King?

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:27.466
-I'm promising you that.

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:31.270
I'm promising you that
the twins don't die together

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.507
with a building falling down
on top of them.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.308
-It would be nice.

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:37.710
If there was a "Game of Thrones"
cross-over,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.211
I think that would be
really nice.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:41.981
And I know you've written the
scripts and everything is done,

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.149
but I think you guys
should try to do that.

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:45.818
-Let's do it. Be happy to.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.953
-Hey, you guys, congratulations.

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:50.156
It's so hard to do
a network show at the level

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:51.590
that you've done it
for so many years,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.193
and I really do hope I see you
next time in-person.

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:56.061
Thank you for being here.
-Thank you.

00:04:56.061 --> 00:04:57.096
-Thank you.
-Thanks for having us, man.

